<td id="S5.T3.13.23.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.294</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">.392</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.799</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.552</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.746</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.643</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.509</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.23.14" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.23.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.623</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.13.24" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">multi-token</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.443</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.303</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.397</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.412</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.291</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.389</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.798</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.595</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.760</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.645</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.523</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.13.24.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T3.13.24.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">.626</span></td></tr>
</table></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Out-of-domain transfer</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to BEIR-7 (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 In-domain fine-tuning ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>),
reading within the same two pairs as in-domain: Dream against
Qwen2.5, and LLaDA against LLaMA3. We report all three
scoring modes (Dense, Sparse, Hybrid) per dataset; the
saturation pattern from §<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 In-domain fine-tuning ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> repeats
out-of-domain (hybrid wins zero-shot, dense wins fine-tuned),
so the discussion below highlights the winning column per
half but the full breakdown is in the table.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Zero-shot transfer.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The in-domain zero-shot pattern from
§<a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Zero-shot in-domain retrieval ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> generalizes to BEIR-7. At
single-token, both diffusion backbones lose: Dream trails
Qwen2.5, and LLaDA is roughly level with LLaMA3. Multi-token
flips both pairs. DiffRetriever Dream multi-token (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".476" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.476</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.476</annotation></semantics></math>) beats
Qwen2.5 multi-token, and DiffRetriever LLaDA multi-token (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".539" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.539</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.539</annotation></semantics></math>)
beats LLaMA3 multi-token and is the strongest zero-shot
BEIR-7 system in our comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-tuned transfer.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the zero-shot half, supervision splits the two pairs.
DiffRetriever Dream multi-token (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".671" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.671</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.671</annotation></semantics></math>) stays ahead of
PromptReps Qwen2.5 multi-token (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".664" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.664</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.664</annotation></semantics></math>) and is the highest
BEIR-7 average in our comparison; the lead is statistically
significant against PromptReps LLaMA3 single-token and
against the encoder-style DiffEmbed Dream baseline (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".638" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.638</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.638</annotation></semantics></math>).
DiffRetriever LLaDA multi-token (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".645" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.645</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.645</annotation></semantics></math>), in contrast, falls
slightly behind PromptReps LLaMA3 multi-token (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".655" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.655</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.655</annotation></semantics></math>).
Dream wins by being steady across datasets, not by dominating
any single one: it holds the column-best score on NQ, FiQA,
and Quora, while AR systems take the other four.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A different way to read the fine-tuned half is by backbone.
Dream-based and AR-pretrained systems all reach the
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".638" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.638</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.638</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".671" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.671</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.671</annotation></semantics></math> band; LLaDA-based systems sit lower, between
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".595" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.595</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.595</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".645" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.645</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.645</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of which retrieval mechanism is
used. LLaDA’s ceiling under contrastive supervision appears
to be a property of the backbone itself, not of how it is
queried at retrieval time. One possible reason: AR
pre-training (or initialization from an AR-pretrained model,
in Dream’s case) builds left-conditional next-token
representations that contrastive fine-tuning can lift
effectively, while LLaDA, trained from scratch as a diffusion
model, may not. Dream’s <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+.195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>.195</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+.195</annotation></semantics></math> BEIR-7 swing from zero-shot
(<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".476" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.476</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.476</annotation></semantics></math>) to fine-tuned (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".671" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.671</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.671</annotation></semantics></math>) is much larger than LLaDA’s
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+.106" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>.106</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+.106</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with this account. We leave verifying
this to future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Across both settings, with a single <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> held
constant across all seven BEIR-7 datasets, multi-token
diffusion gives the strongest retriever in our comparison:
zero-shot LLaDA at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".539" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.539</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.539</annotation></semantics></math>, fine-tuned Dream at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".671" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.671</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.671</annotation></semantics></math>, both
at single-pass query encoding cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Latency analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The teaser figure (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) reports per-query
latency at one operating point: the zero-shot AR multi-token
cap of <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{\leq}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{\leq}20</annotation></semantics></math> on a <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>K-document MS MARCO sample. We
now report the same kind of measurement across two scaling
axes: encoding latency as a function of input sequence
length, and search latency as a function of index size.
Measurements use a single H100 (<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>96</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96</annotation></semantics></math> GB) GPU with the same
attention implementation across all backbones; runs use
synthetic random-token inputs and random-vector indices to
isolate compute cost from retrieval correctness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07210v1/latency_combined.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/211;" width="476" height="211"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Latency scaling on synthetic inputs and indices.
Top row: encoding latency vs. input sequence length
(tokens). Bottom row: search latency vs. index size
(documents, log scale). Left column: PromptReps on
autoregressive backbones (Qwen2.5, LLaMA3). Right
column: DiffRetriever on diffusion backbones (Dream, LLaDA).
Open markers: single-token. Filled markers: multi-token
(AR uses <math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, the fine-tuned cap; diffusion uses the
train-selected <math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>). All measurements
on a single H100 GPU with the same attention
implementation across backbones, <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-query warmup, <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>
timed runs averaged.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4 Latency analysis ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports both axes for the
four main-paper backbones, in two retrieval interfaces
(PromptReps and DiffRetriever), at single-token and
multi-token configurations. The multi-token configurations
in this figure use the fine-tuned cap <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> for AR
backbones (§<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Fine-Tuning Setup ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>) and the train-selected
<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> for diffusion backbones (Dream
<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math>, LLaDA <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Encoding scales mildly with input length on both
families.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the encoding axis (top row of
Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4 Latency analysis ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), all systems scale
roughly linearly with input length, but at very different
absolute levels. AR multi-token sits at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80</annotation></semantics></math> ms
across the range; AR single-token at <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> ms.
DiffRetriever multi-token and DiffRetriever single-token both sit
near <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> ms across the range; the multi-token
overhead is <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> ms on average, far below AR’s
multi-token overhead. At <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> on AR (the fine-tuned cap),
the AR vs. diffusion encoding ratio is <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\approx 3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 3\times</annotation></semantics></math> at
short inputs and narrows toward longer inputs as the
fixed-length passes start to dominate. The teaser ratio
(<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="15\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15\times</annotation></semantics></math>) is at the zero-shot <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{\leq}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{\leq}20</annotation></semantics></math> cap, which
compounds AR’s sequential overhead and is the relevant
operating point for that comparison; at the fine-tuned
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> cap, sequential generation still dominates the gap,
but at a smaller absolute multiple.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Search cost grows with index size and with the
passage budget <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the search axis (bottom row of
Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4 Latency analysis ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), all systems scale
roughly log-linearly with index size on a flat-index ANN
search. Multi-token retrievers have higher search cost than
single-token retrievers because each query performs <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
ANN lookups and the index stores <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> vectors per document.
The difference between DiffRetriever Dream multi-token (red,
top curve, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>) and DiffRetriever LLaDA multi-token
(yellow, <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>) is purely from <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>: Dream’s index is
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger than LLaDA’s at the same number of
documents, which translates directly to higher per-query
search cost. At <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>M documents, the spread between the
slowest multi-token configuration (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 17</annotation></semantics></math> ms,
DiffRetriever Dream) and the fastest single-token configuration
(<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 4</annotation></semantics></math> ms) is well under the encoding-side gap, so
the encoding-cost ratio reported above still dominates
end-to-end latency at corpus sizes up to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>M.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implication.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the deployable AR multi-token cap of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, the
encoding advantage of DiffRetriever over PromptReps narrows
relative to the zero-shot <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{\leq}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{\leq}20</annotation></semantics></math> headline number, but
DiffRetriever multi-token encoding stays comparable to
single-token encoding on either family, while AR multi-token
encoding remains <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> AR single-token across the
entire input-length range. Search cost is small enough at
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>M documents that the encoding-side comparison drives the
end-to-end story; for larger deployments, the larger passage
index of DiffRetriever multi-token (especially Dream’s
<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>) becomes a more substantial component of total
cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Analysis</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main results in §<a href="#S5" title="5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> treated the budget
<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> as a fixed deployment choice, picked once on
MS MARCO training data (train grid in
Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). This leaves two questions:
(1) does the train-selected budget generalize, both to the
MS MARCO dev set and to the seven BEIR-7 datasets? (2) how
much effectiveness does committing to a single budget leave
on the table, compared to choosing <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> per query?
The analysis below answers both. The second answer is worth
flagging up front: a perfect per-query budget predictor on
the frozen base model would exceed contrastive fine-tuning
at the same fixed budget, and the oracle’s per-query
preferences correlate with cheap query features (length and
entropy), suggesting a learned router is reachable
(§<a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Optimal 𝐾_𝑞 correlates with cheap query features ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Generalization of the (<math id="S6.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q},K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q},K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>) landscape</h3>

<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07210v1/kqkp_heatmap_train_horizontal.png" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/228;" width="333" height="228" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Zero-shot hybrid retrieval grid on MS MARCO
train, used for budget selection (§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Budget Selection ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).
Stars mark the per-panel argmax that defines the fixed
<math id="S6.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> used unchanged across all
evaluations: <math id="S6.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math> for Dream and <math id="S6.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaDA.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07210v1/kqkp_heatmap.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:333/309;" width="333" height="309" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Zero-shot hybrid retrieval landscape across
<math id="S6.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on MS MARCO dev
(MRR@10) and BEIR-7 average (NDCG@10), for Dream and LLaDA.
Stars mark the best cell per panel. Train-selected values
(held fixed across evaluations) are <math id="S6.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math> for Dream and
<math id="S6.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaDA (Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two diffusion backbones select different regions of the
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> grid on MS MARCO train
(Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Dream selects
<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})=(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})=(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math>, strongly passage-heavy; LLaDA
selects <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math>, symmetric. Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>
shows the test-set landscape on MS MARCO dev and BEIR-7 for
both backbones, with the best cell on each panel marked.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the train selection generalizes well. On both Dream panels
and on LLaDA’s BEIR-7 panel, the train-selected cell is at
the panel best (sometimes tied with neighboring cells). On
LLaDA’s MS MARCO dev panel, the train-selected <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> is
tied with <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math> at the same score (<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.25</annotation></semantics></math>), within a flat
plateau spanning several cells. The per-backbone asymmetry
also holds across distributions: Dream’s high-score region on
both test panels concentrates around <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}=4</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> on
the larger side, while LLaDA’s grid is more isotropic, with
scores plateauing across a broader region around the
symmetric centre.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The two grids also differ in absolute spread. On MS MARCO
dev, LLaDA cells span <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.18</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.25</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=.07</annotation></semantics></math>); Dream
spans <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.08</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.08</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.22</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=.14</annotation></semantics></math>). A wrong choice of
<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> therefore costs more on Dream than on LLaDA.
This matches the broader pattern from §<a href="#S5" title="5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>:
Dream gains more from good configurations (multi-token,
supervision) than LLaDA does, and equivalently pays more for
poor ones.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Per-dataset (<math id="S6.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q},K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q},K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>) Landscape</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The aggregate panels in
Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compress per-dataset variance.
Figures <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.2 Per-dataset (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) Landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
and <a href="#S6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 6.2 Per-dataset (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) Landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> break the in-domain
and BEIR-7 views into per-dataset panels; both figures
include the corresponding aggregate panel from
Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> as an in-figure reference.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07210v1/per_dataset_heatmaps_indomain.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/331;" width="476" height="331" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S6.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">In-domain per-dataset zero-shot hybrid retrieval
landscape on MS MARCO dev (MRR@10), TREC DL19, and
TREC DL20 (NDCG@10), across <math id="S6.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, for Dream (top row) and LLaDA (bottom row).
Stars mark the per-panel best-performing cell. The
train-selected <math id="S6.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> used in the main
paper is <math id="S6.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math> for Dream and <math id="S6.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaDA.
MS MARCO dev reproduces the corresponding panel from
Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The in-domain panels show a clean pattern. For Dream, the
train-selected <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math> lands at the per-panel best on every
in-domain dataset. For LLaDA, the train-selected <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math>
lands at the per-panel best on DL19, and on the other two
in-domain panels it sits within a flat plateau where multiple
cells tie at the same score. The per-backbone asymmetry from
§<a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> (Dream passage-heavy, LLaDA
isotropic around the symmetric centre) holds in every
in-domain panel.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07210v1/per_dataset_heatmaps_beir7.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/443;" width="476" height="443"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S6.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Out-of-domain per-dataset zero-shot hybrid
retrieval landscape on the seven BEIR-7 datasets and
their average (NDCG@10) across <math id="S6.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, for Dream (top two rows) and LLaDA
(bottom two rows). Stars mark the per-panel
best-performing cell. The train-selected
<math id="S6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> used in the main paper is
<math id="S6.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,16)</annotation></semantics></math> for Dream and <math id="S6.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaDA. The Avg
panel reproduces the BEIR-7 average from
Figure <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> as a within-figure
reference.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The out-of-domain picture is more varied. The aggregate
BEIR-7 generalization claim from
§<a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Generalization of the (𝐾_𝑞,𝐾_𝑝) landscape ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> still holds: the train-selected
cell lands at the per-panel best on the BEIR-7 average panel
for both backbones. Per-dataset peaks, however, scatter
across the grid in a way the aggregate hides. HotpotQA peaks
at <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(8,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(8,16)</annotation></semantics></math> for both backbones, well above the train-selected
score, suggesting that multi-hop QA benefits from larger
budgets on both sides than the train selection allocates.
LLaDA on TREC-COVID peaks at <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where minimal
representations are sufficient. LLaDA on ArguAna and Quora
peaks at the <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}=1</annotation></semantics></math> row, suggesting that short-passage
datasets prefer single passage representatives. The
train-selected cell is rarely more than <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.02</annotation></semantics></math> below the
per-panel best on most datasets, but the per-dataset peaks
themselves can differ from the aggregate by <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.10</annotation></semantics></math> or more
(HotpotQA for both backbones; COVID and Quora for LLaDA).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This per-dataset variance lines up with the per-query oracle
analysis below: a fixed
<math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> leaves substantial performance on the
table when different queries (or different datasets) prefer
different budgets. The aggregate generalization shown by the
average panel is a property of averaging across diverse
preferences, not evidence that any single <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is
universally optimal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Per-query oracle headroom</h3>

<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07210v1/oracle_bars.svg" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/195;" width="476" height="195"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S6.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Per-query oracle headroom on MS MARCO dev (MRR@10)
and BEIR-7 average (NDCG@10), for Dream and LLaDA. Each
group compares the fixed deployable budget
<math id="S6.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> (grey) against three zero-shot
per-query oracles adapting <math id="S6.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> only (blue), <math id="S6.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> only
(red), or both jointly (green), and the contrastively
fine-tuned model at the same fixed budget (orange). Oracles
are upper bounds, not deployable systems: they serve as
ceilings for a learned per-query budget predictor with the
encoder frozen.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to question (2): how much effectiveness is left
on the table by committing to a single <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q}^{*},K_{p}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>
for all queries. Figure <a href="#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.3 Per-query oracle headroom ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> answers this
with three zero-shot per-query oracles (adapting only <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
only <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, or both jointly) and a contrastively fine-tuned
baseline at the same fixed budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The full per-query oracle reaches <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.46</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.46</annotation></semantics></math> MRR@10 on MS MARCO
dev for both backbones, against fixed-budget zero-shot scores
of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.22</annotation></semantics></math> (Dream) and <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.25</annotation></semantics></math> (LLaDA). On BEIR-7 the gap is
<math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.21</annotation></semantics></math> for Dream (<math id="S6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".48\to.68" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>.48</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>.68</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.48\to.68</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.15</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaDA (<math id="S6.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".54\to.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>.54</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.54\to.69</annotation></semantics></math>). Per-query adaptation is therefore a large effect,
with <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> contributing more than <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>: the <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-only
oracle recovers more of the gap than the <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>-only oracle
on every panel, and the full oracle exceeds either partial
oracle, so a per-query router would need to predict both
jointly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The full per-query oracle (green) exceeds the contrastively
fine-tuned model at the same fixed budget (orange) on every
panel: by <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.05</annotation></semantics></math> on MS MARCO dev for both backbones, by <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.03</annotation></semantics></math>
on BEIR-7 for Dream, and by <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.07</annotation></semantics></math> on BEIR-7 for LLaDA. A
perfect per-query budget predictor on the frozen base model
would therefore outperform fine-tuning, with no parameter
updates to the encoder. The <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-only oracle alone almost
matches fine-tuning on BEIR-7 LLaDA (<math id="S6.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.62</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.62</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext=".63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>.63</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">.63</annotation></semantics></math>).
Designing a router that predicts <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> from query
and passage features alone is left to future work; the next
subsection asks whether the oracle’s per-query preferences
are even structured enough to learn from.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Optimal <math id="S6.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> correlates with cheap query
features</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural question is whether the optimal query budget
<math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is predictable from cheap surface features
available without re-encoding. We report two correlations on
the query side: query length and query Shannon entropy, each
for dense and sparse scoring
(Figure <a href="#S6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Setup. ‣ 6.4 Optimal 𝐾_𝑞 correlates with cheap query features ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). We restrict to the
query side because the analysis can be run per query (the
unit at which the oracle in
§<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Per-query oracle headroom ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> operates).</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each query in the pooled MS MARCO dev, DL19, DL20, and
BEIR evaluation sets, we record the per-query peak
<math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (the <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> value that maximizes the corresponding
score on that query, computed in
§<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Per-query oracle headroom ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>). We compute query length as the
model-tokenizer subword count and query entropy as the
Shannon entropy of the query token distribution. We report
Spearman <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and Kendall <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> across the pooled set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07210v1/query_correlation_combined.svg" id="S6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/206;" width="476" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S6.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Per-query peak <math id="S6.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (argmax score over
<math id="S6.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) vs. two cheap query features, on Dream and LLaDA.
Top row: dense scoring. Bottom row: sparse scoring. Left
column: query length (model-tokenizer subwords). Right
column: query Shannon entropy (bits, over tokenizer
ids). Spearman <math id="S6.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and Kendall <math id="S6.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> shown in each
panel inset, with <math id="S6.F9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> bootstrap confidence intervals.
Both features correlate positively with peak <math id="S6.F9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> on
both backbones; length is the stronger signal, and dense
scoring shows cleaner correlations than sparse.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Both features correlate with peak <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, with
length the stronger signal.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Query length correlates with peak <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.31</annotation></semantics></math>
(Dream) and <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.29</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.29</annotation></semantics></math> (LLaDA) on dense scoring
(top-left panel of Figure <a href="#S6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Setup. ‣ 6.4 Optimal 𝐾_𝑞 correlates with cheap query features ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>),
dropping to <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.23</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.23</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.13</annotation></semantics></math> on sparse
(bottom-left). Query entropy correlates slightly less
strongly: <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.28</annotation></semantics></math> (Dream) and <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.25</annotation></semantics></math> (LLaDA)
on dense (top-right), <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.19</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.19</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{=}+0.11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.11</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{=}+0.11</annotation></semantics></math> on
sparse (bottom-right). On the dense panels, peak <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>
rises monotonically from <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> at the shortest queries
to <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> at the longest. On the sparse panels, the
relationship is flatter, especially for LLaDA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dense scoring shows the cleanest signal.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For both features, the dense correlation is consistently
stronger than the sparse correlation. This matches the
saturation observation in §<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 In-domain fine-tuning ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>:
dense vectors carry more retrieval-relevant information per
<math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, so the optimal <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> has more room to scale with input
complexity. Sparse representations saturate at smaller <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,
so the relationship between feature and peak <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is
flatter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implication for adaptive budget selection.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both features are computable from the query alone, without
a forward pass through the encoder, so they are usable by a
learned router that predicts <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> before encoding. The
<math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> values we observe (<math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.11</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.31</annotation></semantics></math>) are not
large, so length and entropy alone would not match the
per-query oracle headroom from
§<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Per-query oracle headroom ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>. They establish that the
oracle’s preferences are at least partially structured
rather than noise, and they are a starting point for the
feature set of a deployable router. We leave the router
itself to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior multi-token variants of PromptReps did not reliably
improve over single-token retrieval despite paying a large
decoding cost, leaving open whether multi-token retrieval is
ineffective or only expensive. We showed that the bottleneck
was sequential generation, not the multi-token idea itself.
DiffRetriever, a representative-token retriever for diffusion
language models, appends <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> masked positions to the prompt
and reads all <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in a single bidirectional forward pass.
Multi-token retrieval then becomes both effective and cheap:
zero-shot DiffRetriever on LLaDA is the highest-scoring system on
BEIR-7 in our comparison, and fine-tuned DiffRetriever on Dream
is the strongest BEIR-7 retriever overall, at single-pass
query encoding cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis further shows that the fixed deployable budget
leaves substantial effectiveness on the table. A per-query
oracle on the frozen base model exceeds contrastive
fine-tuning at the same fixed budget on every
backbone-benchmark combination we test, and the oracle’s
preferences correlate with cheap query features
(§<a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Optimal 𝐾_𝑞 correlates with cheap query features ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>), pointing to adaptive
budget selection as a direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on two diffusion language models (Dream and LLaDA)
at the <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>B parameter scale. Our tightest controlled
comparison (Dream against Qwen2.5) is enabled by Dream’s
initialization from Qwen2.5; analogous controlled comparisons
for other diffusion-autoregressive pairs would require
similarly aligned model families, which are not currently
available for the other backbones we considered.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For out-of-domain evaluation we use BEIR-7, a seven-dataset
subset of BEIR covering open-domain QA, multi-hop QA,
scientific fact verification, biomedical retrieval, financial
QA, argument retrieval, and duplicate-question detection. We
do not run on the remaining BEIR datasets due to compute and
storage constraints: the experiments reported here already
required thousands of GPU hours and over <math id="Sx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> TB of disk
storage for index construction and intermediate artifacts
across the full <math id="Sx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{q},K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{q},K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> grid, four backbones, and both
zero-shot and fine-tuned settings. Extending to the full
BEIR suite would multiply these costs across roughly twice as
many corpora.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The latency comparison in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is reported
on a <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>K-document sample of MS MARCO using the same
attention implementation across backbones, at the zero-shot
<math id="Sx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}20</annotation></semantics></math> generation cap.
§<a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Latency analysis ‣ 5 Results ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> reports encoding and search
latency separately, and across input length, index size, and
the fine-tuned <math id="Sx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}4</annotation></semantics></math> cap; the encoding-cost ratio between
autoregressive and diffusion decoding is what carries our
<math id="Sx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="15\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15\times</annotation></semantics></math> headline claim. At the fine-tuned cap the ratio
is smaller (roughly <math id="Sx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> at short inputs), though
diffusion still encodes faster.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The per-query oracle analysis in §<a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Per-query oracle headroom ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>
uses test labels and serves as a ceiling on what a learned
budget predictor could recover. The query-feature
correlations in §<a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 Optimal 𝐾_𝑞 correlates with cheap query features ‣ 6 Analysis ‣ DiffRetriever: Parallel Representative Tokens for Retrieval with Diffusion Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>
(<math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.31</annotation></semantics></math> on dense scoring) are a step toward
that ceiling but do not match it on their own. Designing a
deployable router that approaches the oracle is left to
future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
